export async function fetchUser(id, { retries = 2 } = {}) {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt <= retries; attempt++) {
    try {
      return await api.get(`/users/${id}`, { timeout: 2000 });
    } catch (err) {
      if (err.name === "TimeoutError") {
        continue;          // swallow the timeout and retry
      }
      throw err;            // any other error still propagates
    }
  }
  return {};                // falls through here once retries are exhausted
}
```

The `for` loop tries the request up to `retries + 1` times (3 attempts by default). Each time `api.get` rejects with a `TimeoutError`, the `catch` block matches `err.name === "TimeoutError"` and does `continue`, silently moving to the next iteration instead of rethrowing. If every attempt times out, the loop simply runs out of iterations and execution falls past the loop to `return {}` on line 12 — there's no `else`/`finally` that rethrows the last timeout error, so the function resolves successfully with an empty object rather than rejecting.

In short: the timeout-retry logic has no "give up and report failure" path — after retries are exhausted it falls through to a hardcoded `{}` instead of throwing the last timeout error (or a wrapped one). Callers that don't inspect the object's shape will treat this indistinguishably from "user has no data."
